Turn a finite Taylor remainder into a justified series representation (@math-series-taylor: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Normalize-countable-weights-without-assigning-probability-from-counts-alone.lingua#r_009B007FED35B6ED29D669B6FA") { r_00495C8F3915BF7C2DBC7E592E
The finite identity in [Bound a finite trigonometric calculation before trusting decimals](Bound-a-finite-trigonometric-calculation-before-trusting-decimals.lingua#r_009546FA47C08EFAEFC38F8134) already gives a polynomial plus an integral remainder for a sufficiently smooth function. To identify the function with an infinite Taylor series, prove that this remainder tends to zero. Merely computing derivatives at one point is insufficient. We use continuous derivatives through the required order on the whole interval, preserving a sufficient hypothesis for the earlier integral argument.

For sine or cosine on |x|≤R, the remainder after degree N is bounded by R^(N+1)/(N+1)!. This tends to zero: for t_k=R^k/k!, the ratio t_(k+1)/t_k=R/(k+1) is at most 1/2 after a finite index, and the geometric comparison controls the remaining sequence. The R=0 case is immediate. Thus the familiar Taylor polynomials converge uniformly on every fixed bounded interval to the already defined geometric sine/cosine functions. A finite interval bound is not a single global error bound over the real line.

The shared [Define exponential change by an inverse and prove its rate law](../../chemistry/core/Define-exponential-change-by-an-inverse-and-prove-its-rate-law.lingua#r_00708D2CC09F8E0AA4DEC25359) defines exp as the inverse of ln and establishes its derivative. On |x|≤R its derivatives are bounded by exp(R), so the same finite remainder argument gives exp(x)=Σ_(n=0 to ∞)x^n/n!, uniformly on each bounded interval. This identifies an independently defined function with its series; it does not assume that identity to prove itself. Taylor approximation and floating-point evaluation remain separate sources of error.

Smoothness alone does not guarantee equality with the Taylor series. Define h(x)=exp(−1/x²) for x≠0 and h(0)=0. Repeated differentiation away from zero gives a polynomial in 1/x times exp(−1/x²). For any fixed power k, |x|^(−k)exp(−1/x²)→0: set t=1/x² and use exp(t)≥t^m/m! for t≥0 with an integer m>k/2, obtained from the proved positive exponential series. The resulting bound m! t^(k/2−m) tends to zero. Inductively the difference quotient at zero also vanishes, so every derivative at zero is zero. Its Taylor series there is identically zero, while h(x)>0 for x≠0. The series converges but fails to represent h away from its center.

**Read:** [Calculus and differential-equation preparation — Strang and Herman, OpenStax](../../chemistry/industrial-chemistry/sources/Calculus-and-Differential-Equations-OpenStax.lingua#r_027WKVM5NG5X84M86TSVRA77J3), Volume 2 section 6.3, especially the distinction between convergence of a series and convergence to the function, and the stated Taylor remainder theorem. Retain the required derivative order; a nearby informal sentence mentioning only n derivatives does not justify a remainder involving derivative n+1. The common proof above uses the already established continuous-derivative integral identity.

**Work:** prove factorial domination with an explicit eventual ratio and turn it into a requested tolerance bound for a chosen R. Explain why all derivatives vanishing at one point does not force an arbitrary smooth function to vanish nearby. Defend every induction step in the h example. Describe separately truncation error, numerical arithmetic error and uncertainty in a physical input represented by x.

**Continue:** [Normalize countable weights without assigning probability from counts alone](Normalize-countable-weights-without-assigning-probability-from-counts-alone.lingua#r_009B007FED35B6ED29D669B6FA).

